%% PHP class interactions — contract chart.
%% At boot, BatterySurfBench loads build/Services.compiled.php (produced
%% ahead of time by ContainerCompiler, run via bin/BuildContainer.php, which
%% precomputes a reflection-free build plan for every class in the plugin's
%% dependency closure, baking each %token% to its configuration value along
%% the way) only outside a development environment — gated via
%% DefinitionsSource against wp_get_environment_type(), so a stale compiled
%% artifact never shadows live edits in local/development — falling back to
%% the live, token-bearing Services.php otherwise, planning each class via
%% reflection on demand. Before ContainerCompiler ever runs, the build
%% validates the definitions via ContainerValidator, which reflects the same
%% closure and collects every structural problem (unknown args, unknown
%% tokens, type mismatches, unmapped interfaces) in one pass; the build
%% aborts with the full error list rather than emitting a compiled artifact
%% when any are found.
%% DevTools boots only where its files are, in a checkout or a development
%% build: the production build leaves them out (see bin/Assemble.mts), so
%% BatterySurfBench checks for DevTools.php before booting it, and DevTools
%% still gates itself to local/development environments.
%% Characters Mermaid would misread inside a label are written as entity codes,
%% which render as the character itself: #58; ':', #59; ';', #40; '(', #41; ')',
%% #123; '{', #125; '}'. See "Keeping Charts Parseable" in CLAUDE.md.
